<Layout title="Projects — Chan Nirak Choun" currentPage="projects">

  <h1>Projects</h1>

  <h2><a href="https://github.com/NirakChoun/KernelForge" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">KernelForge — GPU Kernel Optimization &amp; Performance Analysis</a></h2>
  <div class="entry">
    <div class="entry-subtitle">Sep 2026</div>
    <ul>
      <li>Optimized FP32 SGEMM through six CUDA kernel versions (coalescing, shared-memory tiling, 2D register blocking, vectorized loads) on an RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell, reaching 72% of cuBLAS.</li>
      <li>Wrote memory-bound kernels (reduction, softmax, RMSNorm) in CUDA and Triton at 93–100% of measured DRAM bandwidth; the reduction matched NVIDIA CUB within 0.6%.</li>
      <li>Compared autotuned Triton against hand-written CUDA through PTX/SASS analysis: Triton reached 93% of cuBLAS, but fell to 81% on shapes not divisible by 16 due to narrower loads and register spills.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag">CUDA C++</span>
      <span class="tag">Triton</span>
      <span class="tag">cuBLAS</span>
      <span class="tag">Nsight Systems</span>
      <span class="tag">Slurm</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2><a href="https://github.com/NirakChoun/TensorForge" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TensorForge — MLIR-Based Tensor Compiler for CPU &amp; GPU</a></h2>
  <div class="entry">
    <div class="entry-subtitle">Sep 2026</div>
    <ul>
      <li>Built an MLIR compiler for a custom tensor dialect (TableGen ops, verifiers, folds) lowering through Linalg to x86 and to NVIDIA GPUs via NVVM/PTX, validated with lit/FileCheck tests and FP64 references.</li>
      <li>Generated fused matmul+bias+ReLU GPU kernels with Transform-dialect tiling, shared-memory promotion, and vectorization, matching Triton and beating cuBLAS plus a separate epilogue by 9% on irregular shapes.</li>
      <li>Evaluated an analytical tile-selection cost model against autotuning over 840 runs on two GPU architectures; model picks reached 81% (Blackwell) and 78% (Ampere) of the autotuned best.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag">MLIR/LLVM</span>
      <span class="tag">C++</span>
      <span class="tag">TableGen</span>
      <span class="tag">PTX/SASS</span>
      <span class="tag">Python</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2><a href="https://github.com/NirakChoun/ArchForge" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ArchForge — gem5 Hardware-Software Co-Design Study</a></h2>
  <div class="entry">
    <div class="entry-subtitle">Sep 2026</div>
    <ul>
      <li>Built a gem5 x86 simulation framework (parameterized core and memory configs, 11 self-checking workloads, Slurm sweep pipeline) and ran 495 validated simulations.</li>
      <li>Ran a 190-configuration tile-size × cache co-design study: tiling gave 3.2× vs. 2.5× from a 4× larger L2, joint tuning added only 6%, and tiling cut sensitivity to cache capacity from 2.5× to 1.23×.</li>
      <li>Isolated microarchitectural mechanisms (ROB size vs. memory-level parallelism, prefetching, data layout, false sharing) and root-caused a gem5 artifact: MinorCPU never consults its branch predictor on x86.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="tags">
